---
import LanguageServerDoc from "@components/docs/LanguageServerDoc.astro";
import DocsLayout from "@layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="C#" description="C# language-server support in Axon">
  <LanguageServerDoc
    title="C#"
    description="Axon uses OmniSharp for C# language intelligence. OmniSharp reads solution and project files to provide completions, diagnostics, hover, definitions, references, rename, formatting, and symbols."
    server="OmniSharp"
    delivery="managed native server with local .NET requirement"
    activation="Starts for .cs files and C# workspaces with .sln or .csproj files"
    overview={[
      "C# support depends on the solution or project graph. Target frameworks, NuGet packages, project references, generated files, analyzers, and SDK settings all affect completions and diagnostics.",
      "Axon can bundle OmniSharp, but C# projects commonly still need the local .NET SDK/runtime. The SDK is what understands SDK-style projects, restores packages, and provides framework references.",
      "The most reliable setup is to open the folder that owns the .sln or .csproj files so the server can build the same project graph the developer uses.",
    ]}
    capabilities={[
      "Completions for classes, methods, properties, fields, namespaces, interfaces, and project symbols.",
      "Hover for type information, method signatures, XML documentation, framework APIs, and project declarations.",
      "Diagnostics, definitions, references, rename, formatting, document symbols, and solution-aware navigation.",
      "Better behavior for multi-project solutions when package restore and project references are valid.",
    ]}
    setup={[
      {
        title: "Install the .NET SDK",
        body: "Axon can bundle the server, but real C# project analysis usually depends on a local .NET SDK. Without it, framework references and project loading can fail.",
      },
      {
        title: "Open the solution root",
        body: "Open the folder that owns the .sln or main .csproj. Opening only a nested source folder can make OmniSharp miss references and package metadata.",
      },
      {
        title: "Restore packages",
        body: "Run restore when packages are missing. The language server can only resolve references that exist in the project graph.",
      },
    ]}
    packaging={[
      "OmniSharp is bundled as a managed server for release builds, but the local .NET runtime requirement remains separate.",
      "If Axon reports a generic server failure, logs should show whether the packaged server path is missing or whether .NET/project loading failed after the server started.",
    ]}
    troubleshooting={[
      {
        title: "Framework symbols are missing",
        body: "Install or repair the .NET SDK and restore project packages. Bundling OmniSharp does not bundle every target framework for every project.",
      },
      {
        title: "Only one file has completions",
        body: "The server may not have loaded the solution. Open the root with .sln or .csproj and check LSP logs for project-load errors.",
      },
      {
        title: "Rename or references are incomplete",
        body: "Cross-project features depend on the full solution graph. Missing project references or failed package restore can reduce the result.",
      },
    ]}
  />
</DocsLayout>
